      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the probability that two randomly chosen members from a club of 10 members (5 boys and 5 girls) are both girls.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10. This can be done using the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} \\), which represents the number of ways to choose \\( k \\) items from \\( n \\) items without regard to order. The formula is:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\n\\]\n\nHere, \\( n = 10 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\). So, the total number of ways to choose 2 members from 10 is:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10!}{2!(10-2)!} = \\frac{10!}{2! \\cdot 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of the 5 girls. Using the combination formula again, with \\( n = 5 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2! \\cdot 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4 \\times 3!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n\\]\n\nThe probability that both chosen members are girls is the number of ways to choose 2 girls divided by the total number of ways to choose 2 members from 10. Therefore, the probability \\( P \\) is:\n\n\\[\nP = \\frac{\\binom{5}{2}}{\\binom{10}{2}} = \\frac{10}{45} = \\frac{2}{9}\n\\]\n\nSo, the probability that both chosen members are girls is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{9}}\n\\]",
